  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The District of Columbia saw $23.8B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The District recorded 36,818 awards with an average award size of $646K. Obligations fell 24.3% year over year. The Department of Defense obligated $4.5B, making defense the single largest buying agency in the District for the period. The General Services Administration obligated $3.2B and the Department of Transportation obligated $3.2B. Booz Allen Hamilton Inc is the largest contractor by obligations at $961M, followed by Deloitte Consulting LLP at $837M and Harris Corporation at $732M. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest industry by obligations at $3.4B, followed by Engineering Services at $2.9B and Other Computer Related Services at $2.8B. Fiscal-year comparisons show higher totals in prior years, including $36B in FY24 and $31.4B in FY25, indicating the current FY2026 total is a partial, fiscal year to date figure.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did federal contract spending in the District of Columbia change in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The District of Columbia recorded $23.8B in obligations in FY2026 to date, compared with $31.4B in FY25. Obligations fell 24.3% year over year. The decline follows peak totals such as $36B in FY24 and $33.9B in FY23, showing a lower fiscal year to date total for FY2026."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest federal buyers and vendors in the District of Columbia for FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $4.5B in obligations. The General Services Administration and the Department of Transportation each obligated $3.2B, and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $2.8B. Booz Allen Hamilton Inc is the top vendor at $961M, followed by Deloitte Consulting LLP at $837M and Harris Corporation at $732M."
      }
    ]
  },
